WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:05.160
When Apple announced that you can now get
an 8-core processor in the new 2019 MacBook

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.980
Pro, the first thought that came into everyone’s
mind is that “It’s definitely gonna thermal

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:11.590
throttle if the 6-core MacBook Pro did.

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:15.700
So we’re gonna find out if that’s true
by running our classic Cinebench stress test

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:22.450
on both the new MacBook Pro with the 2.3GHz
8-core processor, 32gigs of RAM and Vega 20

00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:28.099
graphics, as well as on our similarly configured
and priced 2018 model with the 2.6GHz 6-core

00:00:28.099 --> 00:00:29.239
i7 processor.

00:00:29.239 --> 00:00:30.239
$3549.00each

00:00:30.239 --> 00:00:34.800
The reason it’s not the 6-core i9 processor
is because we literally saw zero gains in

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:40.180
video editing compared to the i7, and we expect
the same results with the new 8-core, hence

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:43.980
why we didn’t pay an extra $200 for the
faster 8-core processor.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.859
What we’re gonna do is run Cinebench R20
five times back-to-back to ensure that the

00:00:48.859 --> 00:00:51.559
system heats up and the fans stabilize the
temps, then we’ll compare the average score

00:00:51.559 --> 00:00:52.559
to the 2018 model.

00:00:52.559 --> 00:00:57.520
Shortly after starting our first run, the
CPU boosts up to 4.39GHz, nowhere near the

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:02.109
advertised 4.8GHz turbo boost speed, which
isn’t a surprise because that number is

00:01:02.109 --> 00:01:07.909
rated off just one core out of 8 running by
itself with temps under 50 degrees celsius.

00:01:07.909 --> 00:01:14.450
The clock speed then lowers down to around
3.3GHz just before the temps reach 99 degrees.

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:20.210
As soon as this happens, the wattage drops
to 60, the temps instantly shoot down to 90

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:23.899
degrees, and the clock speed is now sticking
to around 3.0GHz.

00:01:23.899 --> 00:01:29.710
The temp slowly starts picking up again and
stays at around 97 degrees while the clock

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:35.969
speed hovers around 2.9 GHz until we finish
the first run with a score of 3096, before

00:01:35.969 --> 00:01:38.280
instantly rerunning the test again.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:44.750
Just for comparison, the $5000 base iMac Pro
scores around 3,700 in this test.

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:50.530
Starting the second run, the CPU spikes to
a lower 4.31GHz before coming back down to

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:53.659
the same 2.9GHz clock speed range.

00:01:53.659 --> 00:01:58.270
Unlike the first run, the fans are now at
full blast, so it’s able to keep the clock

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:04.000
speed higher at around 3.0GHz at the same
97 degrees temp range, now finishing with

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.689
an even higher 3161 score.

00:02:07.689 --> 00:02:13.110
Starting the third run, the CPU spikes up
to only 3.65GHz, showing the effect temps

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:15.030
have on turbo boost speeds.

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:19.280
Now here we notice something very interesting,
the clock speed is able to stay at around

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:26.460
3GHz while the temps are sitting at a lower
94 to 95 degrees, but out of nowhere, the

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:33.120
CPU clocks down to 2.9GHz even though it doesn’t
need to, since the temps aren’t too high.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:38.760
In fact, they go as low as 91 degrees, with
plenty of thermal headroom for faster clock

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:43.400
speeds, but it doesn’t ever go above 3.0GHz,
almost like it’s being intentionally limited.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:44.400
If it is, maybe it’s due to very careful
thermal management to make sure it gets the

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:45.400
most performance out of the processor, or
there’s always the chance that the 2.4GHz

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:46.400
CPU for $200 more allows the clock speeds
to go up a little bit higher under load, but

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:47.400
who knows.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:48.400
Back to the test, the third run finishes with
an impressive 3125 score.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:53.420
The 4th run spikes up to 3.7GHz turbo boost,
so it seems like the system’s thermals have

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:54.420
stabilized.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:03:00.970
We can now see that the temperature stays
at 93 degrees while at 2.9GHz and at 95 degrees

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:04.709
while at 3.0GHz, now completely stabilized.

00:03:04.709 --> 00:03:12.069
The fourth run scored 3154 points, and the
fifth scored 3147, making for a 5-run average

00:03:12.069 --> 00:03:15.030
score of 3,136.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:18.739
Now before we draw some conclusions and thoughts
from this, let’s quickly talk about the

00:03:18.739 --> 00:03:21.400
results from the 2018 MacBook Pro.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:27.689
Basically every single run hit around 4.03-4.04GHz
peak turbo boost before coming down to around

00:03:27.689 --> 00:03:36.050
3.1-3.3GHz before finally stabilizing at around
3.04-3.08GHz at temps ranging from around

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:42.920
96-97 degrees, and interestingly, we didn’t
see temps randomly going down to 91 or 92

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:45.689
degrees like we did on the 2019 Pro.

00:03:45.689 --> 00:03:51.659
Each run was also very consistent and scored
exactly 2425 points every time.

00:03:51.659 --> 00:03:58.409
Comparing both of them, we see that the 2019
model hit a much higher 4.39GHz turbo boost,

00:03:58.409 --> 00:04:04.750
but the stabilized clock speed was lower at
around 2.9-3.0GHz compared to 3.04-3.08GHz.

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:13.030
However, the stabilized temps were lower staying
between 93 and 95 degrees compared to 96 and

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:14.450
97 degrees.

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:18.610
This shows us that Apple decided to keep the
temps a bit lower on the new model instead

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:22.100
of slightly turning up the power to hit around
97 degrees.

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:26.140
I read through some forums and some users
mentioned that the high heat of previous MacBook

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:30.750
Pros could have been wearing down the components
in the butterfly keyboard, so if that’s

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:34.700
true, it would make sense why Apple would
want the temps to stay lower, and it could

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:37.480
actually fix the keyboard problems once and
for all.

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:41.070
The best part is that even though temps were
lower than they could’ve been, we still

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:45.920
got an average score that’s 30% higher than
the 2018 MacBook Pro.

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:49.920
So we get more power, with cooler temps, at
the same price.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:54.640
Snazzy Labs tested the new 8-core MacBook
Pro and he found that Apple seems to have

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:59.570
set a full load clock speed target, where
instead of turning up the clock speed whenever

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:05.040
there’s extra thermal headroom, the machine
would slow down the fans for a quieter experience.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:09.960
He also replaced the thermal paste with liquid
metal and didn’t find much of a difference,

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:14.820
stating that the 2019 MacBook Pro features
much better thermal paste than previous MacBook

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:15.820
Pros.

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:21.180
This definitely shows in our testing, as the
2019 MacBook Pro idled at only 35 degrees

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:26.970
celcius compared to 45 degrees with the 2018
model, now that’s a huge difference.

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:31.660
We also tested Cinebench R20 using a single
thread to see the difference in performance

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:39.320
and we saw that the 2019 Pro hit a maximum
of 4.5GHz turbo boost compared to 4.17 on

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:45.960
the 2018 Pro, and we scored 466 points compared
to 422.

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:50.490
So not only do we get 2 extra cores, but each
core is faster as well.

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:55.470
So let’s answer our original question, the
2019 8-core MacBook Pro obviously thermal

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:56.800
throttles, right?

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:01.770
No, it actually fixes the throttling issues
from before, performing much better while

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:06.490
at lower temps, which may possibly even fix
the keyboard issues at the same time.

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:10.560
If you’re thankful that we ran these tests
and you’re thinking of buying a 2019 MacBook

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:15.500
Pro for yourself, smash that like button and
